<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- allowBackup=false: everything real lives on the server, so a device
         backup would carry nothing worth restoring — except the WebView's
         cookies, i.e. the Supabase session token, riding into cloud backups
         and device-to-device transfers. Signing in again is cheaper than
         letting an auth token travel. -->
    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme">

        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:exported="true">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!-- The way back in from an OAuth sign-in. THE one the app asks
                 for, as of versionCode 16.

                 Google refuses OAuth inside an embedded WebView, so the
                 consent screen has to open in a real browser; this filter is
                 how the redirect gets back to us. A SEPARATE filter, never
                 merged into the LAUNCHER one above — an activity may carry
                 many, and folding VIEW into MAIN/LAUNCHER breaks the launcher
                 entry.

                 A private-use scheme, not the https App Link below, and the
                 reason is empirical. 15 asked for the App Link; Google's own
                 Digital Asset Links verifier reads our assetlinks.json and
                 parses all three certificates, but the RETURN still depends on
                 Android having run and cached a successful check ON THAT
                 DEVICE — invisible and unforceable from here — and on a real
                 phone it had not. The redirect simply carried on in the
                 browser. A scheme needs no verification at all.

                 What makes the scheme reliable this time is WHERE the consent
                 screen runs: a Chrome Custom Tab launched into this app's own
                 task (oauth-buttons.tsx), not a handoff to the browser app.
                 MainActivity is singleTask, so routing this intent to it
                 brings the task forward and clears the tab off the top on the
                 way — no explicit close, and no chance of the flow continuing
                 somewhere we cannot see.

                 Interception is not a risk: PKCE means the code is useless
                 without the verifier, which is a host-only cookie inside our
                 own WebView.

                 MainActivity is already singleTask and exported, so a resume
                 arrives via onNewIntent and Capacitor turns it into the
                 appUrlOpen event that NativeAuthReturn listens for. -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="ai.picacho.app" android:host="auth-callback" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!-- App Link for the OAuth return. versionCode 15 asked for this
                 URL; 16 does not, and this filter is kept rather than
                 requested — it costs nothing, it still works on a device where
                 verification did land, and a redirect already in flight when
                 16 ships can still arrive on it. Do not make anything depend
                 on it again without a way to observe verification per device.

                 android:autoVerify makes Android fetch
                 https://picacho.ai/.well-known/assetlinks.json at install and
                 check this app's signing certificate against it. Once it
                 verifies, the redirect opens the APP with no chooser and no
                 chance for another app to claim the hop — which is what the
                 custom scheme above could not guarantee.

                 pathPrefix is NOT optional, and it points at /auth/app-
                 callback rather than /auth/callback for a specific reason:
                 /auth/callback is SHARED. Browser Google sign-in returns
                 there, and so does every password-reset email
                 (forgot-password-form.tsx sends ?next=/reset-password).
                 Claiming it would pull those into the app, where the PKCE
                 verifier does not exist — a reset link tapped in Gmail would
                 open the app, fail, and spend its one-time code. The app asks
                 the provider for its own path, and this filter claims only
                 that. Without any prefix it would claim every picacho.ai URL
                 and the marketing site would open in the app as well.

                 www is deliberately absent: next.config.ts 308s it to the
                 apex, and Digital Asset Links verification fails on a
                 redirect. Only the apex is declared, and only the apex serves
                 assetlinks.json. -->
            <intent-filter android:autoVerify="true">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="https" />
                <data android:host="picacho.ai" />
                <data android:pathPrefix="/auth/app-callback" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths"></meta-data>
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!-- Permissions -->

    <!-- Package-visibility declarations (Android 11+). The web app's "Take
         photo" control is <input type="file" capture="environment">, which
         Capacitor CORE serves by resolving MediaStore.ACTION_IMAGE_CAPTURE
         (BridgeWebChromeClient.showImageCapturePicker) — and on API 30+ that
         resolve returns null unless the action is declared here, in which
         case core logs "Media capture intent could not be launched" and
         quietly opens the gallery instead. Until versionCode 12 these three
         rode in on @capacitor/camera's manifest; that plugin was never called
         and is now excluded (capacitor.config.ts), so the declarations live
         where the feature that needs them lives. Measured, not assumed:
         without this block the tap opened the Photopicker; with it, the
         camera. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.media.action.IMAGE_CAPTURE" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.GET_CONTENT" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PICK" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <!-- Android 13+ (API 33) shows no notifications unless the app BOTH
         declares this permission and is granted it at runtime. The runtime
         half is done by the Capacitor push plugin (native-push.tsx calls
         requestPermissions() after sign-in); this declaration is the manifest
         half — without it that runtime prompt is auto-denied and the
         "render finished" pushes silently never arrive on any modern device.
         Neither the plugin's own manifest nor firebase-messaging declares it
         for us. Not declared: CAMERA — reference-photo capture goes through
         the web <input capture> bridge, which delegates to the system camera
         app precisely so no camera permission is needed (see
         BridgeWebChromeClient.isMediaCaptureSupported). -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
</manifest>
